course: dictionary
lesson: 723
title: Words for helpers in court
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name jobs, and the sentences around them tell you what the job does. «A solicitor is a lawyer who gives advice and writes legal papers; a barrister argues cases in court.» The two jobs sit side by side, so the clue is the contrast: one does the paperwork, the other argues.
explain: A barrister is a lawyer who speaks for people in the higher courts and argues their case in front of a judge. To check, swap in your meaning: «a barrister argues a case in court» still makes sense, so you can trust it.
explain: Context also tells you what is not true. «Getting a lawyer does not mean someone did wrong.» You might think a lawyer always means a person who broke the law, but the sentence says no. Read the whole sentence before you guess, because the clues are often there.
explain: Words can change from place to place. In some countries, the word for lawyer is attorney or counsel, so a text from another country may use a different name for a similar helper. When the context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: A barrister is a lawyer who argues cases in court.
fact: «Barrister» means a lawyer who speaks for people in the higher courts, in front of a judge.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, a solicitor usually does the legal paperwork, while a barrister argues the case in court.
fact: In some English courts, barristers wear wigs and robes, a tradition hundreds of years old.
fact: In some countries, the word for lawyer is attorney or counsel.
exercise: «A barrister argues cases in court.» What does a barrister do? | Sells tickets at the door | Looks after the court's animals | Writes the shopping list for the judge | *Argues a person's case in court for them
exercise: In the UK, who usually does the legal paperwork? | A witness in the box | *A solicitor | The judge in her wig | A barrister in the courtroom
exercise: Sam reads «a barrister is a lawyer who argues a case in court». Which other word can mean lawyer in some countries? | Chef | Gardener | *Attorney | Pilot
test: «Getting a lawyer does not mean someone did wrong.» What does this sentence teach us? | Lawyers never go to court | Lawyers only help people who are guilty | *Having a lawyer does not prove that someone did something wrong | Barristers and judges always wear wigs
test: In a court, what is a barrister's main job? | Sitting in the jury box | Writing all the legal papers | Serving the food | *Arguing a case in front of a judge
exam: Read: «In some courts in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, judges and barristers wear wigs and robes.» Which is a good way to check the meaning of barrister? | A barrister is a kind of court building | A barrister is a person who only writes legal papers | *A barrister is a lawyer who argues a case in court | A barrister is a judge who wears a wig
